\chapter{Labels and Translations}
\label{chap:catalogue}

Mentipy puts words of its own next to the lecturer's: the instructions
on the slide (\enquote{Choose exactly one:}), the Submit button and the
headings on the phone, the waiting page, the error pages.  A Swedish
lecture shows Swedish slides, and the phone in a student's hand should
match them.  This module holds those words, in every language Mentipy
ships, and nothing else.

\section{Overview}

One setting, [[mentipy.locale]] (\cref{sec:locale-setting}), chooses the
language for both surfaces.  The two surfaces read it at different
times, which is why the words live in a module of their own rather than
in either of them:
\begin{description}
\item[The slide] The LaTeX helpers (\cref{sec:slide-labels}) read the
  setting when the deck compiles and write each label as a LaTeX macro
  whose default text comes from this catalogue, so a deck can still
  override a single label.
\item[The web pages] The server reads the setting, or the
  [[--locale]] flag of [[mentipy serve]], when it starts, and renders
  every page from the same catalogue.
\end{description}
Both import this module and nothing in it imports them, so the
catalogue can be read, and checked, on its own.  The store module keeps
the setting itself, because every configuration key lives there; this
module only knows the languages.

The catalogue is deliberately not [[gettext]].  [[gettext]] would need
compiled [[.mo]] files in the wheel and a build step to make them, for
two short tables that a translator can read side by side here.  Its
other strength, plural rules beyond \enquote{one or many}, is not needed
by English or Swedish (\cref{sec:catalogue-plurals}).  A plain mapping
also turns the completeness of a translation into a unit test
(\cref{sec:catalogue-tests}).

What never passes through the catalogue matters as much: the question
text, the options and the answers are the lecturer's and the
respondents' words and are shown as they were written.  The catalogue
is also pure presentation.  No question stores a label, so no label
enters [[question_hash]], and changing the language changes no prefix
and no QR code.

