\section{The messages}

Every message has a key, and the key names the surface and the
phrase: [[slide.choose_one]], [[form.submit]].  Each entry holds one
text per locale in the order of [[LOCALES]], so the English text and its
translation sit on the same line.  Adding a language means adding one
element to every entry, and the test in \cref{sec:catalogue-tests}
fails for any entry that was missed.

<<constants>>=
LOCALES = ("en", "sv")
FALLBACK_LOCALE = "en"

MESSAGES: dict[str, tuple[str, ...]] = {
    <<slide messages>>
    <<form messages>>
    <<results messages>>
    <<navigation messages>>
    <<error messages>>
}
@

\subsection{Numbers and plurals}
\label{sec:catalogue-plurals}

A message that carries a number names it as a [[str.format]] field,
[[{n}]], rather than leaving the number to be glued on: the number does
not stand in the same place in every language (\enquote{Rank your top
3} but \enquote{Rangordna de 3 bästa}), and glued strings break as soon
as a translator moves a word.  English and Swedish inflect a counted
noun the same way, singular for one and plural otherwise, so a phrase
whose singular reads differently gets a key of its own ([[pin_one]] and
[[pin_many]]) instead of a plural rule.  A language with more forms
would need a rule here; neither of ours does.  Where the counted noun
is invariant in Swedish (\enquote{280 tecken}) only the English text
uses the plural, which it does for every limit an author sets in
practice.

\subsection{Slide labels}

The slide labels are the respondent instructions the LaTeX helpers
print next to the QR code.  The Swedish follows the wording the intropy
decks already projected (\enquote{Välj ett alternativ:},
[[\item[Svar] Fritext]], [[\item[Högst] N tecken]], [[Skala]],
[[Lägst]], [[Högst]]), so those decks keep their slides when they drop
their own replacement wrappers.  \enquote{Högst} serves both as the
character limit and as the top of a scale, as it did there.

