\subsection{A complete translation}
\label{sec:catalogue-tests}

A translation is complete when every entry has a text for every
locale, and usable when every text takes the same fields as the
English one: a Swedish message missing [[{n}]] would silently drop the
number, and one with a field English lacks would raise a [[KeyError]]
in the middle of a lecture.

<<test functions>>=
def test_every_message_has_one_text_per_locale():
    incomplete = [
        key for key, texts in MESSAGES.items() if len(texts) != len(LOCALES)
    ]
    assert incomplete == []


def test_translations_take_the_same_fields_as_english():
    english = CATALOGUES["en"]
    for locale, catalogue in CATALOGUES.items():
        for key in MESSAGES:
            assert catalogue.fields(key) == english.fields(key), (locale, key)
@


\section{One locale's catalogue}

A [[Catalogue]] is the messages of one locale.  It is called with a key
and the message's fields to get plain text, which suits a page title,
an attribute or a script string, all of which their callers escape as
they would any other text.

Markup is the harder case, because a message often wraps a fragment
the caller has already rendered: the count in \enquote{Total responses:
\textbf{12}}, the prefix in \enquote{No question matches
\texttt{ab12}.}  Escaping the formatted string would escape the
fragment's own tags; not escaping it would let a message's [[<]] or
[[&]] through.  [[markup]] therefore takes the target language's
escape function and walks the message with [[string.Formatter]]: the
message's own text is escaped, and each field is replaced by its
fragment, which is markup already.  A number needs no escaping, and its
format specification ([[{percentage:.0f}]]) still applies.  The same
walk serves the slide, with LaTeX's escape function and [[#1]] as the
fragment (\cref{sec:slide-labels}).

<<classes>>=
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Catalogue:
    """The messages of one locale."""

    locale: str
    messages: Mapping[str, str]

    def __call__(self, key: str, **fields: Any) -> str:
        """Return message `key` as plain text with `fields` filled in."""

        return self.messages[key].format(**fields)

    def markup(
        self,
        key: str,
        escape: Callable[[str], str],
        **fragments: Any,
    ) -> str:
        """Return message `key` as markup.

        The message's own text goes through `escape`; each field is
        replaced by its fragment, which must already be markup (or a
        number, which needs no escaping).
        """

        pieces = []
        for literal, field, spec, _conversion in _FORMATTER.parse(
            self.messages[key]
        ):
            pieces.append(escape(literal))
            if field is not None:
                pieces.append(format(fragments[field], spec))
        return "".join(pieces)

    def fields(self, key: str) -> set[str]:
        """Return the names of the fields message `key` takes."""

        return {
            field
            for _literal, field, _spec, _conversion in _FORMATTER.parse(
                self.messages[key]
            )
            if field is not None
        }
<<constants>>=
_FORMATTER = Formatter()
@

The two ways out of a catalogue: plain text with the fields filled in,
and markup where the message's text is escaped but the fragments are
not.  The format specification of a numeric field survives the markup
walk.

<<test functions>>=
def test_a_catalogue_formats_plain_text_and_escapes_only_its_own_markup():
    catalogue = Catalogue("xx", {"k": "a < b: {n} & {pct:.0f}%"})

    assert catalogue("k", n="<x>", pct=12.6) == "a < b: <x> & 13%"
    assert (
        catalogue.markup("k", html.escape, n="<strong>3</strong>", pct=12.6)
        == "a &lt; b: <strong>3</strong> &amp; 13%"
    )
    assert catalogue.fields("k") == {"n", "pct"}
@


\section{Choosing a locale}

A locale arrives from the configuration or the command line, where
people write it the way their system does: [[sv]], [[sv-SE]],
[[sv_SE.UTF-8]].  Only the language matters for these catalogues, so
the name is reduced to its language code before the lookup.  An unset
locale is English without comment.  A locale Mentipy has no catalogue
for is a typo or a language not shipped yet; neither should stop a
lecture, so it falls back to English with a warning.  The warning comes
once per locale and process: the LaTeX helpers look the setting up for
every question of a deck, and one warning per question would bury the
build log.

<<functions>>=
def normalize_locale(locale: str | None) -> str:
    """Return the language code of `locale`, e.g. `sv` for `sv_SE.UTF-8`."""

    name = (locale or "").strip().lower()
    return name.split(".")[0].replace("-", "_").split("_")[0]


def load_catalogue(locale: str | None = None) -> Catalogue:
    """Return the built-in catalogue for `locale`.

    Unset means English.  An unknown locale falls back to English and
    warns, once per locale.
    """

    name = normalize_locale(locale)
    if not name:
        return CATALOGUES[FALLBACK_LOCALE]
    if name not in CATALOGUES:
        if name not in _WARNED_LOCALES:
            _WARNED_LOCALES.add(name)
            warnings.warn(
                f"Mentipy has no catalogue for locale {locale!r}; "
                f"using English. Known locales: {', '.join(LOCALES)}.",
                stacklevel=2,
            )
        return CATALOGUES[FALLBACK_LOCALE]
    return CATALOGUES[name]
<<constants>>=
_WARNED_LOCALES: set[str] = set()
@

The built-in catalogues are built once, at import, from the table:
column $i$ of every entry becomes the catalogue of [[LOCALES[i]]].  The
mappings are read-only, because every caller shares them.

<<built-in catalogues>>=
CATALOGUES = {
    locale: Catalogue(
        locale,
        MappingProxyType(
            {key: texts[column] for key, texts in MESSAGES.items()}
        ),
    )
    for column, locale in enumerate(LOCALES)
}
ENGLISH = CATALOGUES[FALLBACK_LOCALE]
@

The built-in catalogues answer in their own language, and a locale is
found however the system spells it.

<<test functions>>=
def test_load_catalogue_finds_a_locale_however_it_is_spelled():
    assert load_catalogue("sv")("form.submit") == "Skicka"
    assert load_catalogue("sv_SE.UTF-8").locale == "sv"
    assert load_catalogue(" SV-se ").locale == "sv"
    assert load_catalogue("en_GB")("form.submit") == "Submit"
    assert load_catalogue(None) is ENGLISH
    assert load_catalogue("") is ENGLISH
@

An unknown locale must not stop a lecture: it gives the English
catalogue, and a warning that names the locale, once.

<<test functions>>=
def test_an_unknown_locale_falls_back_to_english_and_warns_once(
    monkeypatch,
):
    monkeypatch.setattr(catalogue_module, "_WARNED_LOCALES", set())

    with pytest.warns(UserWarning, match="'klingon'"):
        assert load_catalogue("klingon") is ENGLISH
    with warnings.catch_warnings():
        warnings.simplefilter("error")
        assert load_catalogue("klingon") is ENGLISH
        assert load_catalogue("sv").locale == "sv"
@
